<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Sacred · 1823 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Himnusz</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem written as a prayer for Hungary in 1823, set to music in 1844, which became Hungary's national anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ferenc Kölcsey</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ferenc Erkel</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Hungarians</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Hungary</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem completed 22 January 1823; music 1844</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Ferenc Kölcsey (written 1823, published 1829); music by Ferenc Erkel (1844))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The poet Ferenc Kölcsey completed the poem on 22 January 1823. The title of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was &quot;Hymnus&quot;, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word for a hymn, and it bore the subtitle &quot;From Stormy Centuries of the Hungarian Folk&quot;. It is often argued that the subtitle, by placing the nation's troubles in the past rather than the present, was added so that the poem would pass the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> censors. The poem was first published in 1829 in Károly Kisfaludy's Aurora, without the subtitle, and appeared with it in a collection of Kölcsey's works in 1832. Its full meaning is evident only to those who know Hungarian history well.</p><p>In 1844 a competition was held for a musical setting that the public could sing, and Ferenc Erkel won it. His version was first performed in July 1844 at the National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a>, where he was conductor, and then before a larger audience on 10 August 1844, at the first voyage of the steamship Széchenyi. By the end of the 1850s it had become customary to sing it on special occasions, on its own or together with Vörösmarty's &quot;Szózat&quot;. From 1844 it served in practice as the hymn of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and over time the Hungarian word himnusz, taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, came to mean a national anthem.</p><p>In the early 1900s members of the Hungarian parliament proposed making its status as the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> official, but no such law was passed. In the 1950s the communist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matyas-rakosi">Mátyás Rákosi</a> planned to replace it with an anthem better suited to communist ideology, but the poet Gyula Illyés and the composer Zoltán Kodály, whom he had in mind for the task, both refused. Only in 1989 did Erkel's setting gain official recognition, when the Constitution of Hungary named it as the national anthem of the Third Hungarian Republic.</p><p>Only the first stanza is sung at official ceremonies. By tradition &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himnusz">Himnusz</a>&quot; opens a ceremony and &quot;Szózat&quot;, often called a second national anthem, closes it. Kossuth Rádió plays it at the close of its AM transmissions each night, the state television channels at the end of the day's broadcasts, and Hungarian television at midnight on New Year's Eve. On 7 May 2006 a sculpture by Mária V. Majzik was inaugurated in Budakeszi, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>: the full text of the poem in a circle around a bronze figure of God, with 21 bells on which the melody can be played, paid for by public subscription.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer to God for the Hungarian people. It looks back over the nation's misfortunes across the centuries and asks God's blessing on them; the mood is solemn and grave, and the melody resembles a Protestant chorale.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hungarian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hungarian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Isten, áldd meg a magyart</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God, bless the Hungarian</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Jó kedvvel, bőséggel,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With good cheer, with plenty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Nyújts feléje védő kart,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Stretch out a protecting arm towards him</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Ha küzd ellenséggel;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When he fights with the enemy;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Bal sors akit régen tép,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Him whom ill fate has long been tearing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Hozz rá víg esztendőt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bring upon him a merry year,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Megbűnhődte már e nép</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This people has already atoned</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">A múltat s jövendőt!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the past and the future!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Őseinket felhozád</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our ancestors you brought up</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Kárpát szent bércére,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Onto the holy peaks of the Carpathians,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Általad nyert szép hazát</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Through you a fair homeland was won</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Bendegúznak vére.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the blood of Bendegúz.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">S merre zúgnak habjai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And wherever the waves roar</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Tiszának, Dunának,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of the Tisza, of the Danube,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Árpád hős magzatjai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Árpád's heroic offspring</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Felvirágozának.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Burst into flower.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 64 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Értünk Kunság mezein</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For us on the fields of Kumania</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Ért kalászt lengettél,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You waved the ripe ears of grain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Tokaj szőlővesszein</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the vine shoots of Tokaj</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Nektárt csepegtettél.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You dripped nectar.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Zászlónk gyakran plántálád</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our flag you often planted</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Vad török sáncára,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the wild Turk's ramparts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">S nyögte Mátyás bús hadát</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And under Matthias's grim army groaned</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Bécsnek büszke vára.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Vienna's proud castle.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Hajh, de bűneink miatt</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Alas, but because of our sins</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Gyúlt harag kebledben,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Anger kindled in your breast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">S elsújtád villámidat</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And you struck down your lightnings</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Dörgő fellegedben,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From your thundering cloud,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Most rabló mongol nyilát</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now the plundering Mongol's arrows</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Zúgattad felettünk,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You sent whirring above us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Majd töröktől rabigát</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then from the Turk a slave's yoke</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Vállainkra vettünk.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We took upon our shoulders.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Hányszor zengett ajkain</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How often there sounded on the lips</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Ozmán vad népének</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of Osman's wild people,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Vert hadunk csonthalmain</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Over the bone heaps of our beaten army,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Győzedelmi ének!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A song of victory!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Hányszor támadt tennfiad</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How often your own son rose</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Szép hazám, kebledre,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Against your breast, my fair homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">S lettél magzatod miatt</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And you became, because of your offspring,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Magzatod hamvvedre!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The urn of your offspring's ashes!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Bújt az üldözött, s felé</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The persecuted one hid, and towards him</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Kard nyúl barlangjában,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A sword reaches into his cave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Szerte nézett s nem lelé</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He looked all around and did not find</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Honját a hazában,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>His home in the homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Bércre hág és völgybe száll,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He climbs to the peak and goes down to the valley,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Bú s kétség mellette,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Grief and doubt beside him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Vérözön lábainál,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A flood of blood at his feet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">S lángtenger felette.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And a sea of flame above him.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Vár állott, most kőhalom,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A castle stood, now a heap of stones,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Kedv s öröm röpkedtek,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Gladness and joy flitted about,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Halálhörgés, siralom</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The rattle of death, lamentation</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Zajlik már helyettek.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now surge in their place.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">S ah, szabadság nem virúl</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And ah, freedom does not bloom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">A holtnak véréből,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the blood of the dead,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Kínzó rabság könnye hull</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tears of tormenting slavery fall</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Árvák hő szeméből!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the burning eyes of orphans!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Szánd meg Isten a magyart</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have pity, God, on the Hungarian</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Kit vészek hányának,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whom storms of peril have tossed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Nyújts feléje védő kart</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Stretch out a protecting arm towards him</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Tengerén kínjának.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the sea of his torment.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Bal sors akit régen tép,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Him whom ill fate has long been tearing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Hozz rá víg esztendőt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bring upon him a merry year,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">Megbűnhődte már e nép</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This people has already atoned</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hu">A múltat s jövendőt!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the past and the future!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ferenc Kölcsey (1823). Text from <a href="https://hu.wikisource.org/wiki/Himnusz_(K%C3%B6lcsey_Ferenc)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>The poem addresses God throughout; 'you' and 'your' are God, except in stanza 5, lines 5-8, which turn to the homeland.</li><li>line 2.4: Bendegúz is the legendary father of Attila, whom Hungarian tradition took as an ancestor.</li><li>line 2.7: Árpád led the Hungarians into the Carpathian Basin (c. 895).</li><li>line 3.1: Kunság (Cumania) is the plain between the Danube and the Tisza settled by the Cumans.</li><li>line 3.7: Mátyás is King Matthias Corvinus, who took Vienna in 1485; 'bús' usually means 'sad', here grim or fierce [?].</li><li>line 5.2: 'Ozmán' is Osman, founder of the Ottoman dynasty; his people are the Ottoman Turks.</li><li>line 5.5: 'tennfiad' (your own son) refers to Hungarians who fought against Hungarians.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
